= Shoyu

_Scope: This entry treats_ shoyu _(醤油, Japanese soy sauce) — the canonical Japanese fermented soybean-and-wheat-and-salt liquid seasoning, descended from the Chinese_ 醬油 (jiàngyóu) _tradition. Like_ miso _(see_ miso.typ_), shoyu is_ post-classical _in the strict Bencao / classical-tradition sense: the_ Shennong Bencao Jing _and the canonical 本草 corpus treat 醬 (jiàng, the fermented bean paste) and 豆豉 (dòuchǐ, fermented black soybeans) but_ not _the strained liquid 醬油 / shoyu form, which is a Song-era (10th–13th c.) Chinese development substantively elaborated in Japan from the Kamakura period onwards. Status: not canonical Bencao; classical in Japanese dietetics; not classical in Ayurveda or Tibb; central in modern East Asian and globalized cuisine; substantial modern fermentation-science and nutrition documentation. Closely related to_ miso _(味噌) — both share the_ kōji _(Aspergillus oryzae) fermentation; see_ miso.typ_._

== Nomenclature & Etymology

*Latin / botanical:* Shoyu is *not a plant* — it is a *fermented sauce* made principally from *_Glycine max_* (L.) Merr. (soybean, Fabaceae) and *_Triticum aestivum_* L. (wheat, Poaceae), plus *salt* and a *kōji starter* — *_Aspergillus oryzae_* Ahlb. (the koji mold, the same organism used in sake, miso, and rice vinegar fermentation). Modern food-science taxonomy: shoyu is a *brewed, double-fermented soy sauce* in the *Pacific (Asian) sauce* category, distinct from Western Worcestershire-type sauces. The fermentation involves two stages: (1) *koji culture* — A. oryzae growing on steamed soybeans and roasted wheat for 2–3 days; (2) *moromi fermentation* — the koji mixed with brine, with the principal fermentation by *Tetragenococcus halophilus* (lactic acid) and *Zygosaccharomyces rouxii* (yeast), over 6 months to 2+ years.

*Japanese:* *醤油 (shōyu, romanized shoyu)* — Modern Japanese; the characters 醤 ("sauce, paste, fermented condiment" — the Japanese reading of Chinese 醬) + 油 ("oil, liquid"). The name literally "sauce-liquid" — distinguishing the liquid strained form from the paste-form *miso* (味噌). The earliest Japanese attestations of *liquid shoyu*-style sauce are 13th-c. — the canonical origin story attributes shoyu to *Buddhist monk Kakushin* (覚心, also Hotto Kokushi), who returned to Japan from the Song-Chinese province of Hangzhou in 1254, founding the temple at Yuasa (Wakayama prefecture) and reportedly introducing the *kinzanji miso* technique, from whose moromi the liquid shoyu form was developed. Yuasa remains a historic shoyu-production center. By the Edo period (1603–1868), the regional Japanese shoyu types had developed: *koikuchi shoyu* (濃口, dark/standard — central and eastern Japan, ~80% of national production); *usukuchi shoyu* (淡口, light — Kansai / Kyoto, lighter color but actually saltier); *tamari shoyu* (溜まり, wheat-free or nearly so, from miso byproduct — Chubu region, Aichi); *shiro shoyu* (白, white — almost no soybean, principally wheat — Aichi); *saishikomi shoyu* (再仕込み, twice-brewed — Yamaguchi / Hiroshima, intense flavor).#footnote[Etymology and history: standard Japanese food-history literature; the Kakushin / Yuasa origin story is the canonical traditional account, well-documented in Yuasa town historical records and in Shurtleff & Aoyagi, _History of Soy Sauce_ (Soyinfo Center). The 13th-c. introduction date is preserved in monastic records.]

*Chinese:* *醬油 (jiàngyóu)* — the modern Chinese name for shoyu / soy sauce, literally "sauce-oil" — paralleling the Japanese. The Chinese 醬油 form is a *Song-era development* (10th–13th c.) from the older 醬 (jiàng) paste tradition; the *strained liquid soy sauce* as a distinct preparation is *not in the Han-Tang 本草* but emerges in the late-medieval Chinese culinary tradition. Modern Chinese types: *生抽 (shēng chōu, "fresh draw," light soy sauce)*; *老抽 (lǎo chōu, "old draw," dark soy sauce — molasses-darkened, used for color)*; *頭抽 / 頭抽油 — first-pressed*; *草菇老抽 — straw-mushroom-dark*; multiple regional and brewing variants. The Cantonese tradition has a particularly elaborated dark/light distinction; the Sichuanese 醬油 tradition leans towards 老抽 for color and 生抽 for seasoning; the Shanghainese cuisine uses heavy dark 老抽 for the canonical "red-cooking" (紅燒 hóng shāo) style.

*Korean:* *간장 (ganjang)* — the Korean parallel; *조선 간장 (Joseon-ganjang)* is the traditional Korean soy sauce produced as the *liquid byproduct of doenjang (Korean miso) fermentation* — preserving the older Chinese 醬 paste-and-liquid linked-fermentation tradition. Modern Korean *왜간장 (waeganjang)* refers to Japanese-style koikuchi-equivalent commercial shoyu.

*Indonesian / Malaysian:* *kecap (Indonesian)* — the *kecap* family includes *kecap manis* (sweet, palm-sugar-thickened), *kecap asin* (salty, plain), and others; Indonesian *kecap* is the *etymological source of the English "ketchup"* (via Malay / Hokkien Chinese sailors' adoption — the name traveled from Chinese 鮭汁 _kê-chiap_ "brine of pickled fish" via Malay to English, where it eventually denoted the tomato-based condiment).

*Sanskrit / Arabic / Latin classical:* *Not classical in Ayurveda, Tibb, or Greco-Roman traditions.* Like miso, shoyu is an *East Asian fermented sauce* outside the canonical reach of the western and South Asian classical pharmacopoeias.

== Classical Chinese Medicine (中醫) View

*The strained liquid shoyu / 醬油 form is not in the classical Bencao*. The *parent 醬 (jiàng) paste tradition is classical Chinese* (Zhou dynasty onwards — see `miso.typ` for the parent-category discussion), and the closely-related *豆豉 (dòuchǐ, fermented black soybeans) IS canonical Bencao* (_Shennong Bencao Jing_; the Han-dynasty Shanghan-Lun drug 淡豆豉 dàn dòuchǐ).

For *the parent 醬 / 豆豉 axis as the foundation for reading shoyu by extension:*
- *Property:* warm (溫) for long-fermented dark koikuchi; near-neutral for short-fermented usukuchi.
- *Flavor:* salty (鹹), with sweet (甘) and umami notes.
- *Channels:* Spleen (脾), Stomach (胃), Kidney (腎).
- *Actions (by extension):* harmonizes Stomach; mild deobstruent; salty descending and softening; modest cold-dispersing (for warmer koikuchi types).
- *Cautions:* heavy salt for hypertensive patients; damp-heat patterns (the salty-warming-fermented register).#footnote[The Chinese 醬 / 豆豉 parent tradition: see `miso.typ` for the parent-category discussion. The strained 醬油 / shoyu form is *post-Bencao* and the reading by extension applies the classical principles to the modern liquid form — flagged as extension, not canonical citation.]

The *clinical / dietetic register* for shoyu is essentially *cooking-condiment* in Chinese tradition; the herb has no significant medical formulary use comparable to drugs like 豆豉 or 昆布. Modern Chinese dietetic literature treats shoyu in the same register as miso (see `miso.typ`).

== Ayurvedic Dravyaguna (द्रव्यगुण)

_Shoyu is not classical in Ayurveda._ The reading would follow the same logic as for miso (see `miso.typ`): salty-warming-fermented profile with snigdha-umami quality; Vāta-Kapha-pacifying / Pitta-aggravating; post-classical extension only. The substance is outside the classical Indian foodscape.

== Traditional Arabic & Islamic Medicine (Ṭibb al-ʿArabī wa-l-Islāmī / Unani)

_Shoyu is not classical in Tibb._ Soybeans and Japanese fermented preparations are outside the canonical reach of the Arabic-Islamic medical tradition; no Avicennan, Ibn al-Bayṭār, or Tibb al-Nabawī attestation.

== Classical Formulas & Preparations

=== Chinese medicine (中醫)

No classical formulas use the strained liquid 醬油 form. The parent 豆豉 appears in classical Han-dynasty formulas (*梔子豉湯 Zhī Zǐ Chǐ Tāng*, _Shanghan Lun_) — see `miso.typ` for the discussion.

=== Japanese (the canonical home tradition)

*Shoyu is the foundational liquid seasoning of Japanese cuisine*, central in:
- *Daily kitchen seasoning* — used in virtually every savory dish in Japanese cooking; the *base of the sauce trinity* (shoyu + mirin + sake) that defines Japanese flavor.
- *Sushi and sashimi dipping* — _murasaki_ (the colloquial name for shoyu when served with sushi) is the canonical dipping sauce.
- *Tsuyu (つゆ) and dashi-based sauces* — shoyu + dashi + mirin + sake — the universal Japanese sauce-and-broth base, used for soba dipping, tempura dipping, simmered dishes (nimono), and oden.
- *Teriyaki* (照り焼き) — shoyu + sugar + mirin glaze for grilled fish and chicken.
- *Tsukudani* — small fish, kombu, or other ingredients simmered in shoyu and mirin as a relish.
- *Sukiyaki, shabu-shabu* — shoyu-based hot-pot seasonings.
- *Pickling* — shoyu-based pickle bases (shoyu-zuke).
- *Soba and udon broths* — shoyu + dashi as the foundation.

=== Regional Japanese typology

- *Koikuchi (濃口)* — the standard dark shoyu; ~80% of national production; central and eastern Japan. Universal kitchen seasoning.
- *Usukuchi (淡口)* — the light Kansai shoyu; *actually saltier* than koikuchi despite the lighter color; used in Kyoto cuisine where the goal is to season without darkening the ingredient color (preserving the elegant Kyoto kaiseki aesthetic).
- *Tamari (溜まり)* — wheat-free or nearly so; descended from the liquid that pools above miso during fermentation; deeper umami; the Chubu region (Aichi) speciality; *the natural gluten-free shoyu* — though traditional tamari uses some wheat, modern "tamari" is often labeled as gluten-free.
- *Shiro (白)* — white shoyu; ~90% wheat, ~10% soybean; very pale color; sweet, light flavor; used in seafood preparations where shoyu color would be visually intrusive.
- *Saishikomi (再仕込み)* — "twice-brewed"; the moromi is fermented with shoyu instead of brine, doubling the umami and creating an intensely flavored finishing sauce; Yamaguchi / Hiroshima regional speciality.

=== Brewed (honjozo) vs. chemical (HVP) — the quality distinction

- *Honjozo (本醸造)* — traditionally brewed via koji and moromi fermentation, 6 months to 2+ years; the classical product.
- *HVP / chemical soy sauce* (アミノ酸液 / 醤油風味調味料) — *hydrolyzed vegetable protein*: defatted soybean (or other protein) treated with hydrochloric acid, neutralized, blended with caramel color and flavorings. *Not fermented; chemically distinct; nutritionally distinct from real brewed shoyu*. Some commercial products are *blends* of honjozo and HVP. *The quality distinction is critical*: the honjozo product carries the modest probiotic, polyphenolic, and bioactive-peptide register of fermentation; the HVP product does not. *Always check the label*: traditional brewed shoyu lists soybean, wheat, salt, koji (or A. oryzae) — and nothing else.

== Climate, Constitution & Regional Diet

Shoyu is a *cool-temperate East Asian seasoning* with the same climatic profile as miso (see `miso.typ`). The Yuasa / Wakayama and Noda / Choshi traditional production regions reflect the cool-winter / hot-summer continental seasonal-extreme pattern of central and eastern Honshu; the long moromi fermentation is climatically apt for the cellar-stored cool-winter / warm-summer cycle.

=== Continental seasonal-extreme + cool-damp (Japan, Korea, eastern China)

The home climate. Daily use of shoyu is climatically and constitutionally universal in Japanese cuisine, with regional intensity matching local climate: *salty Kanto koikuchi* for cold-winter eastern Japan; *lighter Kansai usukuchi* for milder central Japan and Kyoto's refined cuisine; *intense saishikomi* for the cool-coastal Inland Sea regional palette.

=== Modern globalized use

Shoyu has spread globally; the modern Western use is climatically agnostic. The high sodium concern is the principal modern dietary consideration; the quality distinction (honjozo brewed vs. HVP chemical) is the principal modern *quality* consideration.

=== Modern transposition (_deśa-viruddha_)

The modern globalized "soy sauce" register is universally adopted across all climates without seasonal or constitutional adjustment; sodium load and product quality are the principal modern caveats. The traditional Japanese seasonal logic — *darker koikuchi in winter, lighter usukuchi in summer, lighter Kyoto cuisine in warm seasons* — is largely lost in modern global use. See Caraka Sū. 26 for the _viruddha_ framework; see `climates.typ` for the cool-damp and continental seasonal-extreme profiles.

== Modern Nutrition & Pharmacology

*Approximate composition (typical brewed koikuchi shoyu, per 100 g):*
- Energy ~53 kcal; Water ~71 g (shoyu is mostly water, despite the intense flavor)
- Carbohydrate ~5 g (residual sugars from fermentation); fiber ~0 g (filtered out)
- Protein ~8 g (mostly free amino acids — high glutamate, the umami source)
- Fat ~0.1 g (negligible)
- *Sodium very high* — ~5500 mg per 100 g (~5.5% by weight; *the principal modern nutritional consideration*; the sodium content is largely responsible for shoyu's preservation properties)
- Minerals: modest iron, magnesium, manganese
- *Free amino acids — very high*: glutamate (~1500 mg per 100 g, the umami source), aspartate, alanine, leucine, etc.
- *Isoflavones* (soybean phytoestrogens) — modest amounts; the koji fermentation increases bioavailability
- *Melanoidins and Maillard products* — from the long fermentation and (in some commercial products) sugar caramelization; antioxidant register
- *Bioactive peptides* — from koji-mediated soybean protein hydrolysis; ACE-inhibitory and antioxidant activity in some studies
- *Biogenic amines* — histamine, tyramine, putrescine — small amounts in fermented shoyu; *relevant to MAOI medication interactions*
- *Polyphenols, flavonoids* — modest amounts from the soybean and wheat starting material#footnote[Phytochemistry and nutrition: extensive modern Japanese and Korean fermentation-science literature; standard references in _Journal of Agricultural and Food Chemistry_, _Food Chemistry_, _Journal of Functional Foods_; the brewed (honjozo) vs. HVP distinction is the principal quality lever.]

*Pharmacology of note:*
- *Glutamate / umami* — the principal sensory and nutritional component; the *umami principle of Japanese cuisine* is anchored in shoyu (and kombu, see `kombu.typ`).
- *Antioxidant melanoidins* — modest clinical relevance; supports daily-intake epidemiology of fermented foods.
- *ACE-inhibitory peptides* — modest blood-pressure-lowering effect in some studies, despite the high sodium content of shoyu itself.
- *Anti-Helicobacter activity (in vitro)* — some fermentation-derived peptides show H. pylori inhibition; clinical translation unclear.
- *Allergy* — soybean and wheat allergies; tamari (low-wheat) is appropriate for wheat-sensitive (though "tamari" labeled products vary).
- *3-MCPD and chloropropanols* — *modern food-safety concern* — HVP / chemical soy sauce production using hydrochloric acid can generate 3-monochloropropane-1,2-diol (3-MCPD) and related chloropropanols, which are *carcinogenic and renal-toxic*. The EU and Asia food-safety authorities have regulated these contaminants; *traditional brewed (honjozo) shoyu has effectively no 3-MCPD*; chemical and blended products historically had higher levels (substantially reduced by modern regulation). The honjozo / chemical distinction is *not just a quality / flavor issue but a food-safety issue*.

*Safe daily dosage / upper limit:*
- *Traditional Japanese culinary use:* ~10–30 g of shoyu per day, divided across multiple dishes; this is the canonical Japanese culinary intake.
- *Sodium limit:* the principal practical limit; 1 tablespoon (~15 g) of shoyu = ~825 mg sodium; hypertensive patients should moderate.
- *Pregnancy:* generally safe at typical culinary intake.
- *MAOI medication:* avoid (biogenic amines).
- *Soy allergy:* avoid; wheat allergy may use traditional tamari (verify labeling).
- *Sourcing:* *honjozo brewed shoyu, not HVP* — the food-safety, quality, and nutritional considerations all favor traditional brewed product.
- *Reference figures, not personalized medical advice.*#footnote[Dose ranges from standard Japanese nutritional literature; sodium considerations standard. 3-MCPD considerations: EFSA scientific opinions; ASEAN food-safety standards; traditional brewed shoyu is generally free of this concern; verify product labeling.]

